      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess the association between obesity and parental coronary heart disease (CHD) history."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Analysis of data from an ongoing, large-scale survey on medical status, health behaviour and attitudes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Representative samples of Israeli military personnel upon discharge from compulsory service at age 20-22 y. Overall 14297 men and 11638 women were interviewed and examined upon release from military service between 1989 and 1999."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "Data on demographic characteristics, family history of CHD, lifestyle, weight and height were collected. Analysis of variance and logistic regression were used."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Higher mean body mass index (BMI) and obesity (BMI>30 kg/m(2)) were associated with paternal CHD history in both sexes, and with maternal CHD history among men. Offspring of a parent with a positive CHD history had a higher mean BMI (23.22 vs 22.86 kg/m(2), P<0.001) and were more likely to be obese (5.4 vs 3.7%, P<0.001) than offspring of parents with no history of CHD. Multivariate adjustment for demographic and behavioural variables associated with obesity attenuated the association (adjusted odds ratio for obesity 1.37, 95% confidence interval: 1.15, 1.64). When stratified by sex, this association remained statistically significant only among males."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Young adults with a parental history of CHD are more likely to be overweight. This high-risk group should be targeted for early preventive activities."
        }
      ]},
